        "summary": "# COMP2105: Operating Systems – Disk Scheduling Algorithms (Lecture 7-8)\n\nThis lecture covers disk structure fundamentals and various disk scheduling algorithms to minimize seek time and improve performance.\n\n## Mass Storage Structure\n- **Magnetic disks**: Primary secondary storage.\n- Rotation: 60-250 RPS.\n- **Transfer rate**: Data flow speed between drive and computer.\n- **Positioning time**: Seek time (move arm to cylinder) + rotational latency (sector under head).\n- **Head crash**: Head contacts platter → data loss.\n- Connected via I/O buses: EIDE, ATA, SATA, USB, SCSI, etc.\n\n## Disk Scheduling\nGoal: Optimize servicing of I/O requests to reduce total head movement (seek time dominant).\n\n**Disk bandwidth** = bytes transferred / time from first request to last completion.\n\nStandard queue example: Requests 98,183,37,122,14,124,65,67; head starts at 53.\n\nWalk-through example: Requests 90,180,30,12,10,130,60,80; head at 50.\n\n### Algorithms Comparison\n| Algorithm | Description | Total Movement (Standard) | Total Movement (Walk-through) | Notes |\n|-----------|-------------|---------------------------|-------------------------------|-------|\n| **FCFS**  | Order of arrival | 640 | 510 | Simple, can have high movement |\n| **SSTF**  | Shortest seek from current | 236 | 300 | Good movement, risk of starvation |\n| **SCAN**  | Elevator: one direction to end, reverse | 208 | 210 | Fairer, bounded wait |\n| **C-SCAN**| Circular: service one way, jump back | 322 | 320 | More uniform wait times |\n| **C-LOOK**| Like C-SCAN but to farthest request | 322 | 320 | Optimizes C-SCAN by avoiding ends |\n\n- **SSTF**: Greedy, minimal movement but starvation possible.\n- **SCAN/C-SCAN**: Bounded wait, C-SCAN more uniform.\n- **C-LOOK**: Variant of C-SCAN, moves only to last request.\n\nChoosing algorithm depends on workload; real systems use variants (e.g., LOOK).",
