        "summary": "# COMP2105: Operating Systems – Main Memory Management (Lecture 9)\n\nThis lecture covers basic memory management techniques: swapping for handling larger process sets than physical memory and dynamic partition allocation strategies.\n\n## Memory Swapping\n- Processes can be temporarily **swapped out** to **backing store** (fast disk) and back in.\n- Enables total process memory > physical memory.\n- **Backing store**: Holds full memory images with direct access.\n- **Roll out/roll in**: Variant for priority scheduling – swap out lower-priority to load higher-priority.\n- Swap time dominated by **transfer time** (proportional to swapped memory size).\n- Ready queue includes processes with images on disk.\n\n## Multiple-Partition Allocation\n- Memory divided into partitions (some allocated, some free **holes**).\n- OS tracks allocated partitions and free holes.\n\n## Dynamic Storage Allocation\nWhen allocating a process to a hole:\n- **First-fit**: First hole large enough (fastest).\n- **Best-fit**: Smallest hole that fits (search entire list, smallest leftover).\n- **Worst-fit**: Largest hole (search entire list, largest leftover).\n\n### Example (Holes: 100,500,200,300,600 KB; Processes: 212,417,112,426 KB)\n- **First-fit**: P4 waits (max leftover <426).\n- **Best-fit**: All fit (P4 in remaining 600 → 174 leftover).\n- **Worst-fit**: P4 waits.\n- **Best-fit** most efficient here (no waiting processes).\n\nThese strategies trade off speed, memory utilization, and fragmentation. No single best in all cases.",
