        "summary": "# COMP2220: Web Information Systems - Lecture 1: World Wide Web\n\nThis lecture provides an introduction to Web Information Systems (WIS), starting with fundamental concepts and progressing to core features, architecture, and protocols of the Web.\n\n## Outline\n- **Internet vs. Web**: Clarifies the distinction, noting that the Web is a subset of the Internet.\n- **Information vs. Data**: Defines information as processed, organized, and structured data presented in context.\n- **Information System (IS) vs. Information Technology (IT)**: IT encompasses hardware and software, while IS includes hardware, software, people, procedures, and data.\n- **Web Information System (WIS) and Applications**: Explores what a WIS is and its various uses, such as eBusiness, online reservations, and banking.\n- **Internet vs. Intranet**: Differentiates between the public Internet and a private, local network (intranet).\n- **Webpages and Websites**: Covers the basics of static and dynamic websites.\n- **Basics of Web Technology**: Introduces core features like URLs, HTTP, Web Server Software, HTML, and Browsers.\n\n## Key Concepts\n- **Internet vs. Web**: The Internet is a vast network of networks, while the World Wide Web (WWW) is a system of interlinked hypertext documents accessed via the Internet. The Web is merely one of the many services available on the Internet, alongside email, instant messaging, and FTP.\n- **Data vs. Information**: Raw data becomes information when it is processed, organized, structured, or presented within a given context, making it useful.\n- **IT vs. IS**: Information Technology (IT) refers to the hardware and software components needed for a system to operate. An Information System (IS) is a broader concept, including IT along with people, procedures, and data, all working together to deliver information and processing functions.\n- **Web Information System (WIS)**: An information system that leverages Internet web technologies to deliver information and services. Examples include online news, eBusiness platforms, reservation systems, and online banking.\n- **Client-Server Model**: In a web context, a server maintains data/information, and a client (e.g., a web browser) provides an interface to access and present this data.\n- **Tim Berners-Lee**: Credited with the invention of the WWW, proposing a Hypertext System in 1990 at CERN.\n- **Core Features of the Web**: These include:\n    - **URL (Uniform Resource Locator)**: Uniquely identifies resources.\n    - **HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol)**: Defines how requests and responses operate.\n    - **Web Server Software**: Responds to HTTP requests.\n    - **HTML (Hypertext Markup Language)**: Used to publish documents.\n    - **Browser**: A program to make HTTP requests and display HTML.\n- **Web Apps vs. Desktop Apps**: \n    - **Advantages of Web Apps**: Accessible from any internet-enabled device, usable across different OS and browser platforms, easier updates (server-side), centralized storage.\n    - **Disadvantages of Web Apps**: Requires active internet connection, security concerns for sensitive data transmission, storage concerns, potential display issues across browsers.\n- **Intranet**: A private network local to an organization, accessible only to authorized users. The Internet is a broader term encompassing both private (intranet) and public networked resources.\n- **Static vs. Dynamic Websites**:\n    - **Static Websites**: Contain fixed HTML content, display the same information to every visitor, and do not require web programming or database design.\n    - **Dynamic Websites**: Display varied content each time they are browsed, with content created at run-time by programs, and often require web programming or database design.\n- **Web 2.0 and Beyond**:\n    - **Web 2.0**: Focused on interactive experiences, user contribution, and collaboration. For developers, it involved new paradigms for dynamic websites, requiring technologies like JavaScript.\n    - **Web 3.0**: Envisions machines being able to read data.\n- **Multi-tiered Architecture**: Discusses 2-tiered (server and client) and 3-tiered architectures (web server, application server, database server) for web applications.\n- **Domain Name System (DNS)**: Maps human-readable domain names to IP addresses. It is structured hierarchically, read from right to left, with each section denoting a subdivision (e.g., TLD, SLD).\n- **Uniform Resource Locator (URL)**: A web address specifying the location of a resource on the internet, composed of protocol, domain, path, query string, and fragment.\n- **Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP)**: Establishes TCP connections (default port 80) and defines how messages are formatted and transmitted between clients (HTTP Requests: GET, POST) and web servers (HTTP Responses).\n- **Web Requests**: A single web page loading involves multiple HTTP requests for the initial HTML, then subsequent requests for linked resources like images, stylesheets, and scripts. The page is fully loaded when all resources are retrieved.\n- **GET vs. POST Requests**: Illustrates how GET requests are typically used for retrieving information (e.g., hyperlinks) and POST requests for submitting data (e.g., form data).\n- **Web Server**: A computer storing web server software and website files, responding to HTTP requests. A Web Stack includes an operating system, web server software, a database, and a scripting language.",
