        "summary": "# COMP2222: Introduction to Computer Animation\n\nThis lecture provides a foundational overview of animation, tracing its origins, evolution, core principles, and production processes. It emphasizes that computer animation builds directly on the principles established in traditional 2D animation, particularly those popularized by Disney and later formalized for CGI by John Lasseter at Pixar in 1987.\n\n## What is Animation?\nAnimation creates the illusion of movement by rapidly displaying sequential images. This relies on **persistence of vision**, where the brain blends fast images (typically 24 frames per second in film) into continuous motion.\n\n## History of Animation\n### Early Devices (1800s)\nBefore cameras, mechanical devices created motion illusions:\n- **Thaumatrope**: Spinning disk combining two images into one.\n- **Flipbook**: Rapid page flipping.\n- **Zoetrope/Phénakistoscope/Praxinoscope**: Rotating cylinders/drums with slits showing one frame at a time.\n\n### Traditional Animation\n- Hand-drawn frames, often using stop-motion or camera tricks (e.g., Georges Méliès).\n- Early problem: Every frame redrawn entirely → slow and expensive.\n\n### Cel Animation (Key Breakthrough)\n- Invented by Earl Hurd (1914).\n- Characters on transparent cels over reusable backgrounds.\n- Enabled layering, peg systems, camera panning, rotoscoping.\n- Standard: 24 fps (1,440 frames per minute).\n\n### Walt Disney's Innovations\n- Emphasis on personality and storytelling.\n- Storyboards, synchronized sound (Steamboat Willie).\n- Multiplane camera for depth.\n- Eventually transitioned to digital systems in the 1990s.\n\n### From Cel to Digital\nDigital animation uses the **same principles** but replaces physical drawings/cameras with software, offering faster editing and greater flexibility.\n\n## Principles of Animation\nThese 12 principles (originally from Disney, applied to CGI by John Lasseter) form the 'grammar' of animation:\n\n| Principle                  | Description                                                                 |\n|----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Squash and Stretch         | Shows weight/flexibility; deform objects while preserving volume.           |\n| Timing                     | Controls speed; conveys weight, emotion, and realism.                       |\n| Anticipation               | Prepares viewer for action (e.g., pull back before punch).                  |\n| Staging                    | Presents ideas clearly; directs attention (one idea at a time).             |\n| Follow-Through & Overlapping Action | Parts stop at different times; appendages drag/follow (realistic motion).   |\n| Slow In and Slow Out       | Eases acceleration/deceleration for natural movement.                       |\n| Arcs                       | Movements follow curved paths (splines) for lifelike trajectories.          |\n| Exaggeration               | Amplifies actions/emotions for impact; must remain balanced and believable. |\n| Secondary Action           | Small supporting actions that reinforce the main action.                    |\n| Appeal                     | Characters should be interesting, clear, and likable (avoid symmetry, add detail). |\n| Solid Drawing              | Maintain 3D volume, weight, and balance in drawings.                        |\n| Personality                | Unique application of principles to reflect individual character mood/attitude. |\n\n(Note: Straight-ahead vs. pose-to-pose is often discussed as a technique within these principles.)\n\n## Animation Production\n### Traditional Pipeline\n1. Story & Script\n2. Storyboard\n3. Model Sheets (character consistency)\n4. Keyframes\n5. In-betweens\n6. Inking/Coloring on Cels\n7. Sound (pre- or post-recorded for lip-sync)\n8. Editing (assembly of shots/sequences)\n\n### Modern Computer Animation\n- Same foundational steps (storyboarding, keyframing).\n- Divided into departments: Story/Art, Modeling/Layout, Animation, Lighting/Rendering.\n- Advantages: Non-linear editing, reusable assets, easy effect adjustments.\n\nThis introduction sets the stage for understanding how timeless animation principles translate seamlessly into the digital realm, enabling the expressive and realistic computer-animated films we see today.",
